1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:02,902
Eleven fifty eight in the morning. Moscow, Idaho.

2
00:00:02,902 --> 00:00:08,039
A nine one one dispatcher picks up a call from a rented house on King Road.

3
00:00:09,166 --> 00:00:10,625
A young woman was crying.

4
00:00:10,625 --> 00:00:14,417
Something has happened in our house, she said, and we don't know what.

5
00:00:15,566 --> 00:00:20,199
Another voice took the phone. One of the roommates is passed out, she said.

6
00:00:20,199 --> 00:00:21,522
She's not waking up.

7
00:00:22,666 --> 00:00:27,081
And then, almost as an afterthought, one line that changed everything that followed.

8
00:00:27,081 --> 00:00:30,137
They saw some man in their house last night.

9
00:00:31,266 --> 00:00:37,300
By the time that call was placed, four University of Idaho students had been dead for roughly eight hours.

10
00:00:38,433 --> 00:00:42,665
Their names were Kaylee Goncalves, Madison Mogen, Xana Kernodle and Ethan Chapin.

11
00:00:42,665 --> 00:00:48,307
This film is about the record their case produced, and what that record still doesn't say.

12
00:00:49,433 --> 00:00:53,025
Almost everything the public knows about this case came from documents.

13
00:00:53,025 --> 00:00:55,964
Not from a trial, because there never was one.

14
00:00:57,100 --> 00:01:00,917
A probable cause affidavit. A plea agreement. A judgment.

15
00:01:00,917 --> 00:01:06,008
And, four years later, a hand-written petition from a maximum security prison.

16
00:01:07,133 --> 00:01:10,245
Those four documents are the entire spine of this episode.

17
00:01:10,245 --> 00:01:14,290
Where they are silent, we will say so, and we will stop there.

18
00:01:15,433 --> 00:01:21,232
The first document, the affidavit, describes a night in a rented house three blocks from campus.

19
00:01:22,366 --> 00:01:26,754
Investigators placed the killings between about four and four twenty five in the morning.

20
00:01:26,754 --> 00:01:28,635
Nobody called for help at four.

21
00:01:29,766 --> 00:01:33,572
One housemate told investigators she woke to sounds from a room upstairs.

22
00:01:33,572 --> 00:01:35,793
She opened her door more than once.

23
00:01:35,793 --> 00:01:43,088
On one of those occasions, she described standing in frozen shock as a masked figure in black walked past her toward a door.

24
00:01:44,233 --> 00:01:46,717
She was twenty years old. She had been asleep.

25
00:01:46,717 --> 00:01:50,581
Every account of what she did next has to start with those two facts.

26
00:01:51,700 --> 00:01:58,621
For nearly eight hours, the house was quiet, and the people still alive inside it believed the people who weren't were sleeping.

27
00:01:59,766 --> 00:02:05,017
Before any of that, the neighbourhood's own cameras recorded something nobody noticed for weeks.

28
00:02:06,166 --> 00:02:11,924
Footage pulled from multiple cameras around King Road showed the same car, repeatedly, in the hour before the killings.

29
00:02:11,924 --> 00:02:15,257
The first sighting was at three twenty nine in the morning.

30
00:02:16,400 --> 00:02:17,941
The affidavit puts it plainly.

31
00:02:17,941 --> 00:02:24,106
Those sightings show the vehicle making an initial three passes by the house, before a final one at four twenty.

32
00:02:25,233 --> 00:02:30,066
Fifty one minutes. A car circling a street where four students were asleep.

33
00:02:31,200 --> 00:02:36,020
And then, according to cell site records obtained months later, the same phone came back.

34
00:02:36,020 --> 00:02:41,806
At approximately nine that morning, it left the area of the Kohberger residence and travelled to Moscow again.

35
00:02:42,933 --> 00:02:50,639
Five hours after the killings, and roughly three hours before anyone called nine one one, the record places that phone back in the same town.

36
00:02:51,766 --> 00:02:57,252
The case turned on a single object, left behind in the room where two of the victims were found.

37
00:02:58,400 --> 00:03:03,389
A tan leather knife sheath. A Ka-Bar sheath, lying beside Madison Mogen.

38
00:03:04,533 --> 00:03:06,934
The knife itself has never been recovered.

39
00:03:06,934 --> 00:03:12,422
The sheath was what mattered, because of a detail almost too small to picture. The snap.

40
00:03:13,566 --> 00:03:19,474
The Idaho State Lab found a single source of male DNA on the button snap of that sheath.

41
00:03:19,474 --> 00:03:21,115
One person. Not a mixture.

42
00:03:22,233 --> 00:03:26,036
And here is the problem investigators had in November of twenty twenty two.

43
00:03:26,036 --> 00:03:29,547
They had a profile. They had no name to attach it to.

44
00:03:30,666 --> 00:03:36,387
What happened next is the part of this case that will outlive it, because it is now used across the country.

45
00:03:37,533 --> 00:03:42,084
No criminal database contained that profile. So the FBI went somewhere else.

46
00:03:42,084 --> 00:03:47,015
Public genealogy databases, where ordinary people upload their own DNA to find relatives.

47
00:03:48,133 --> 00:03:50,425
The technique does not look for the suspect.

48
00:03:50,425 --> 00:03:56,727
It looks for his cousins, and then it builds family trees outward from them, until the branches close in on one household.

49
00:03:57,866 --> 00:04:00,551
That household was in Monroe County, Pennsylvania.

50
00:04:00,551 --> 00:04:06,304
In late December, investigators collected trash from outside the home of a family named Kohberger.

51
00:04:07,433 --> 00:04:18,039
Comparing what they found there to the profile from the sheath, the lab concluded that a male in that household could not be excluded as the biological father of the person who left DNA on that snap.

52
00:04:19,166 --> 00:04:21,691
Prosecutors later put a number in the record.

53
00:04:21,691 --> 00:04:27,057
At least five point three seven octillion times more likely to be his than an unrelated person's.

54
00:04:27,057 --> 00:04:29,267
That is the figure in the filing.

55
00:04:29,267 --> 00:04:34,317
It is not our claim, and it is not a statement about guilt on its own.

56
00:04:35,466 --> 00:04:40,064
DNA identified a person. Two other kinds of record put him in motion.

57
00:04:41,199 --> 00:04:44,990
The first was a white Hyundai Elantra, a twenty fifteen model.

58
00:04:44,990 --> 00:04:48,435
Investigators traced its movements on the morning of the thirteenth.

59
00:04:49,566 --> 00:04:56,678
Five days after the killings, that car was re-registered in Washington state, and given a new licence plate.

60
00:04:56,678 --> 00:04:59,048
Before that, it carried Pennsylvania plates.

61
00:05:00,166 --> 00:05:01,976
The second record was a phone.

62
00:05:01,976 --> 00:05:10,119
According to the affidavit, his phone had connected to towers in the area of that house at least twelve times before that night, beginning the previous August.

63
00:05:11,266 --> 00:05:17,749
He was a doctoral student in criminal justice at Washington State University, ten miles away, across the state line.

64
00:05:17,749 --> 00:05:22,185
He was arrested in Pennsylvania on December thirtieth, six weeks after the killings.

65
00:05:23,333 --> 00:05:27,695
By the middle of December, the car everyone was looking for was crossing the country.

66
00:05:28,833 --> 00:05:33,833
On December thirteenth, a licence plate reader in Loma, Colorado captured it.

67
00:05:33,833 --> 00:05:37,584
Two days later, law enforcement in Hancock County, Indiana.

68
00:05:38,733 --> 00:05:47,484
On December sixteenth, at about two twenty six in the afternoon, surveillance video showed the Elantra in Albrightsville, Pennsylvania, where his family lived.

69
00:05:48,633 --> 00:05:53,544
Two thousand five hundred miles, tracked afterward, from records that already existed.

70
00:05:54,666 --> 00:05:59,746
On December twenty third, investigators obtained search warrants for the historical records of that phone.

71
00:05:59,746 --> 00:06:04,148
Everything the affidavit says about where it had been comes from those warrants.

72
00:06:05,266 --> 00:06:09,501
He was arrested in Pennsylvania on December thirtieth, twenty twenty two.

73
00:06:09,501 --> 00:06:12,580
Six weeks and four days after the killings.

74
00:06:13,700 --> 00:06:17,191
Two details in the file sit oddly beside all of it.

75
00:06:17,191 --> 00:06:22,588
He had been stopped in traffic in Moscow before, and again in October by a university officer.

76
00:06:22,588 --> 00:06:28,302
And he had posted a public research survey online, asking offenders about how they had chosen their targets.

77
00:06:29,433 --> 00:06:31,211
He was a criminology doctoral student.

78
00:06:31,211 --> 00:06:36,249
That is in the record too, and it is the kind of detail that invites a theory.

79
00:06:36,249 --> 00:06:38,027
The record does not supply one.

80
00:06:39,166 --> 00:06:43,920
Now the part of the record that lawyers, not viewers, tend to notice.

81
00:06:45,066 --> 00:06:48,549
The affidavit that justified his arrest described the DNA comparison.

82
00:06:48,549 --> 00:06:54,470
It did not mention that genetic genealogy had been used to find him in the first place.

83
00:06:55,600 --> 00:06:58,142
The lead detective later acknowledged that omission.

84
00:06:58,142 --> 00:07:03,228
Prosecutors confirmed the technique in filings months afterward, in June of twenty twenty three.

85
00:07:04,366 --> 00:07:07,736
The defence spent much of the next two years attacking exactly that.

86
00:07:07,736 --> 00:07:13,352
What the databases were, whose rules applied, and what a judge should be told about how a suspect was found.

87
00:07:14,500 --> 00:07:18,706
That argument was never resolved by a jury, because the case never reached one.

88
00:07:19,833 --> 00:07:24,431
Between the arrest and the plea, the case was fought almost entirely on paper.

89
00:07:25,566 --> 00:07:29,389
The defence challenged the genealogy. They challenged the warrants that followed it.

90
00:07:29,389 --> 00:07:37,034
They asked what databases had been searched, under whose terms of service, and what the judge who signed the arrest warrant had been told.

91
00:07:38,166 --> 00:07:43,540
In February of twenty twenty four, a court took argument specifically on the genetic genealogy.

92
00:07:43,540 --> 00:07:48,197
Whether the technique was reliable was barely the point. The question was disclosure.

93
00:07:49,333 --> 00:07:56,804
None of it was ever decided by a jury, because in July of twenty twenty five the defendant removed the jury from the equation entirely.

94
00:07:57,933 --> 00:08:04,202
On July second, twenty twenty five, two and a half years after the killings, he pleaded guilty.

95
00:08:05,333 --> 00:08:08,872
Four counts of first degree murder, and one count of burglary.

96
00:08:08,872 --> 00:08:12,412
In exchange, the state took the death penalty off the table.

97
00:08:13,533 --> 00:08:22,127
Three weeks later, on July twenty third, he was sentenced to four consecutive life terms without the possibility of parole, plus ten years for the burglary.

98
00:08:23,266 --> 00:08:27,237
He was given the opportunity to speak at that hearing. He declined.

99
00:08:28,366 --> 00:08:34,016
Which means the families left that courtroom with a sentence, a signed agreement, and no stated reason.

100
00:08:34,016 --> 00:08:36,342
There is no motive in this record.

101
00:08:36,342 --> 00:08:40,330
Anyone who tells you otherwise is filling a silence with a guess.

102
00:08:41,466 --> 00:08:44,567
For a year, that was the end of it.

103
00:08:44,567 --> 00:08:48,702
Then, on July twenty seventh, twenty twenty six, a new filing arrived.

104
00:08:49,833 --> 00:08:55,793
A petition for post conviction relief, filed from the Idaho Maximum Security Institution.

105
00:08:55,793 --> 00:08:59,002
Hand-written. And filed by him, representing himself.

106
00:09:00,133 --> 00:09:02,591
In it he asks for a new trial.

107
00:09:02,591 --> 00:09:10,582
He alleges that his lawyers failed him, and that his guilty plea was not knowing or voluntary, because it was induced, he writes, by unkept promises.

108
00:09:11,700 --> 00:09:16,775
Specifically, he claims he was promised things about prison life that did not follow.

109
00:09:16,775 --> 00:09:18,225
Contact visits. Movement. Work.

110
00:09:18,225 --> 00:09:23,664
And that he was told what he calls elaborate lies about life on death row.

111
00:09:24,800 --> 00:09:26,926
Every one of those claims is his.

112
00:09:26,926 --> 00:09:32,396
None of them has been tested, and the person making them has an obvious interest in the outcome.

113
00:09:32,396 --> 00:09:36,346
That is not a dismissal. It is the standard the record itself applies.

114
00:09:37,466 --> 00:09:44,212
Legal commentators have generally described his chances as poor, and pointed to the appeal waivers inside the agreement he signed.

115
00:09:44,212 --> 00:09:48,934
That is commentary, not a ruling. The petition is live as this is published.

116
00:09:50,066 --> 00:09:54,215
One of the mothers said publicly that her family is ready for a trial.

117
00:09:54,215 --> 00:09:58,660
After four years of documents, that sentence carries a weight nothing in the filings does.

118
00:09:59,800 --> 00:10:02,748
Idaho, like every state, has a procedure for this.

119
00:10:02,748 --> 00:10:05,042
Post conviction relief is not an appeal.

120
00:10:05,042 --> 00:10:11,268
It is a separate action, with its own deadline, and its own burden on the person who files it.

121
00:10:12,400 --> 00:10:22,169
To undo a guilty plea this way, a petitioner generally has to show the plea was not knowing, not voluntary, or that his lawyers failed him in a way that changed the outcome.

122
00:10:22,169 --> 00:10:24,834
Asserting it is not the same as showing it.

123
00:10:25,966 --> 00:10:29,971
And the agreement he signed contained waivers limiting exactly this kind of challenge.

124
00:10:29,971 --> 00:10:33,359
That is the wall his own filing has to get over.

125
00:10:34,500 --> 00:10:38,753
So this is what the record holds. A sheath with one man's DNA on the snap.

126
00:10:38,753 --> 00:10:42,741
A car photographed going out and coming back. A phone that had been there before.

127
00:10:42,741 --> 00:10:46,464
A signature on an agreement. And a hand-written page asking to take it back.

128
00:10:47,600 --> 00:10:53,791
And one thing it does not hold, anywhere, in four years and thousands of pages. A reason.

129
00:10:54,933 --> 00:10:58,591
Four students went to sleep in a rented house on a Saturday night.

130
00:10:58,591 --> 00:11:01,124
The document that explains why has never been filed.

131
00:11:02,266 --> 00:11:06,718
This is Criminal Record, where every case is built from the primary record and nothing else.

132
00:11:06,718 --> 00:11:12,561
If this one held your attention, the next file we open is the one where the paperwork itself was the crime.

133
00:11:12,561 --> 00:11:14,230
Subscribe, and you'll see it first.

